<document lang="en" type="paper">
  <title>Decision Trees</title>

  <intro>
    A classification tree partitions the feature space by applying tests
    at internal nodes. A leaf predicts a class from the samples that
    reach it. This document describes the criteria used by
    <c>ml_library.models.decision_tree</c>.
  </intro>

  <section id="gini">
    <title>Gini</title>

    <subsection id="definition">
      <title>Definition</title>

      Let $S$ be the set of sample indices at a node, with labels
      $y_i \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$. For each class $k$, let
      $\hat p_k(S)=|\{i\in S:y_i=k\}|/|S|$ be its empirical frequency.
      The Gini impurity of a nonempty node is

      <equation id="eq:gini">
        G(S) = 1 - \sum_{k=1}^{K} \hat p_k(S)^2.
      </equation>

      For two independent draws $Y,Y'$ from the empirical class
      distribution, $G(S)=\Pr(Y\ne Y')$. A pure node has $G(S)=0$.
      In the binary case, with class frequencies $p$ and $1-p$,
      $G(S)=2p(1-p)$, which is maximal at $p=1/2$.

      The function <c>_gini</c> computes $G(S)$ from the label counts.
    </subsection>

    <subsection id="split">
      <title>Split criterion</title>

      A candidate $(j,t)$ consists of a feature index $j$ and a threshold $t$.
      For a sample $x_i$, the test $x_{ij}\le t$ sends $i$ to the left
      child $L$. The remaining indices form the right child $R$.
      For two nonempty children, <c>_split_gini</c> computes

      <equation id="eq:split">
        Q_S(j,t) = \frac{|L|}{|S|}G(L) + \frac{|R|}{|S|}G(R).
      </equation>

      For each feature, <c>_best_split</c> evaluates midpoints between
      consecutive distinct values and selects the valid candidate that
      minimizes $Q_S(j,t)$. Since $G(S)$ is fixed at the parent node,
      this is equivalent to maximizing $G(S)-Q_S(j,t)$.
    </subsection>

    <subsection id="example">
      <title>Worked example</title>

      Suppose $S$ contains six samples of class A and four of class B.
      Then $G(S)=1-(6/10)^2-(4/10)^2=0.48$. Consider a split that sends
      four A samples to $L$ and leaves two A and four B samples in $R$.
      Hence $G(L)=0$ and $G(R)=1-(2/6)^2-(4/6)^2=4/9$.

      <equation id="eq:example">
        Q_S(j,t)=\frac{4}{10}\cdot 0+\frac{6}{10}\cdot\frac{4}{9}
        =\frac{4}{15}\approx 0.267.
      </equation>

      If the classifier selects this split, the left leaf predicts A
      and the right leaf predicts B by majority vote.
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section id="classifier">
